From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:30:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 30/35] Delay: end of the repeats counted by the right buffer,
 tail fade that always fits, Tape for old files with a modulated rate

- Fade mode: while a change to a shorter time fades, the repeats are counted
  by the old buffer (the one the sound still goes round), not the new one.
  Counting the new, shorter buffer gave up before the old one had played out:
  at feedback 0.2 and 0.5 -> 0.12 s after the notes, the second repeat
  (-28 dB) was missing; at 0.5 and 1 -> 0.05 s the echo on its way was cut
  off. Now the same as with no countdown. A change to a longer time still
  counts the new buffer; an LFO on the time, which keeps the delay fading,
  still lets it give up.
- End of the repeats: the fade of the last time round is set each block to
  reach 0 where the counting buffer comes round (samplesUntilWrap()), or a
  time round later where that would be steeper than full level in 1024
  samples; hasWrapped() waits for it. Before, it assumed a whole round and
  cut off at full echo level when the last round began partway (feedback
  below 3 %, or turned down after note-off): -33.8 dB, now below -110 dB.
- Below 3 % feedback the delay no longer gives up while sound comes in:
  1.2.1 discarded the buffer at every time round while playing, so its
  repeats were cut off a block after they came round (tape mode too).
- Files from before v14 (no timeChange): a sound with a patch cable to the
  delay rate (LFO, envelope, ...) gets Tape, so its modulation doesn't turn
  into steps; others Fade. Decided when the sound first renders or its delay
  menu opens (Sound::resolveDelayTimeChange), as a song's patch cables are
  only read with its clips; picking a value in the menu settles it. Song,
  kit and audio track delays have no patch cables and keep Fade.
- Note (verifier finding 3): with the 4 s buffers, tape mode also differs
  from v13 for delays between 2 and 4 s (a buffer of their own instead of
  one resampling at 2 s).

